<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerard of Cremona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1114–<a href="/years/1187">1187</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerard of Cremona was an Italian scholar who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and translated scores of Arabic scientific and philosophical works into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. His translations included Ptolemy's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>'s Canon of Medicine, works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a>, and the surgery of al-Zahrawi. They made him the most productive of the twelfth-century translators who transmitted Greek and Arabic learning to Latin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona.jpg" alt="Gerard of Cremona" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerard of Cremona · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-RaziInGerardusCremonensis1250.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
